package teyru

/* java.security.MessageDigest and the checksum interface java.util.zip declares,
   with the hash functions themselves written here as plain Teyru arithmetic
   rather than reached for through the runtime. The runtime has a SHA-1 -- the
   WebSocket handshake needs one -- and it is deliberately not on this side of
   the language: a digest a program can ask for by name is a digest whose
   intermediates have to exist where the program can see them.

   What is here. MessageDigest.getInstance answers for MD5, SHA-1, SHA-224,
   SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512: the SHA-2 family is one block function over 32
   or 64 bit words, so the four SHA-2 sizes fall out of two implementations and
   two initial values, and MD5 is the other block function the JDK ships.
   CRC32 is java.util.zip's reflected CRC-32, over the same Checksum interface
   that class implements, and it is the second half of this file because a
   digest and a checksum are asked the same question by the same kind of
   caller.

   What is not here, and why each is a decision rather than an omission.

   * The JDK at 21 also answers for SHA3-256 and its siblings and for
     SHA-512/256 (and SHA-512/224). getInstance throws NoSuchAlgorithmException
     for those instead of answering with a different digest: a digest that is
     silently not the one the caller named is the worst thing this file could
     do, while a name that is not known is a compile-time-sized problem a
     caller can see.
   * getInstance(String, String) and getInstance(String, Provider) are absent,
     for the reason the rest of this library has no Provider: there are no
     providers to name, and a method that ignored its second argument would be
     answering a question it had not read.
   * clone() is absent. Java's MessageDigest clone copies the digest state and
     throws CloneNotSupportedException for the algorithms that cannot be copied
     mid-stream; nothing in this library clones, and an object that can be
     copied is an object whose invariants have to be written twice.
   * update(ByteBuffer) is absent because there is no ByteBuffer in this
     library, and a method that took an Object and hoped would be worse than a
     method that does not exist.
   * toString() is not overridden. Java's carries the provider's name and the
     number of bytes that have been fed; there are no providers here, and the
     runtime's Object.toString is what a program printing one gets.
   * CRC32C, Adler32 and the Deflater are not here. CRC32C is a different
     polynomial rather than a flag on this one, and Adler32 belongs to a
     compression stream this library does not have.

   The one structural difference from Java is that MessageDigest is concrete
   with a private constructor rather than abstract over a MessageDigestSpi.
   Nothing observable follows from it -- getInstance is the only way to a
   MessageDigest in either language -- but it is a difference, so it is named.

   How this file is verified. tests/programs/t170_digest.teyru is the same
   program as the javac 21 program in this file's .java.ref, whose output is
   tests/programs/t170_digest.expected: the empty message, one byte, "abc", the
   messages that straddle the 55/56 byte and 111/112 byte padding boundaries,
   a message that is mostly a block, a multi-block message fed a byte at a time
   and as a slice, one digest carried into the next by digest(byte[]), reset
   and reuse, and -- for SHA-384 and SHA-512 -- messages that straddle the
   128 byte block. Every hex string printed there is the JDK's own answer for
   that input, which is the only oracle that can say whether the round
   constants, the padding and the byte order are right.

   tests/programs/t171_crc32.teyru is the same arrangement for CRC32, against
   the same javac. What neither test can reach: a digest of a message larger
   than the counters, and the messages of the failures (an unknown algorithm
   name, a null array, a digest buffer too short) which are checked but printed
   through getMessage() alone, because a Teyru exception's toString carries
   this runtime's package where Java's carries java.security's. */

class GeneralSecurityException extends Exception {
  public GeneralSecurityException() {
  }
  public GeneralSecurityException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

class NoSuchAlgorithmException extends GeneralSecurityException {
  public NoSuchAlgorithmException() {
  }
  public NoSuchAlgorithmException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

/* What digest(byte[], int, int) throws when the caller's buffer cannot hold the
   digest. Java declares it on that one method and nowhere else in this file. */
class DigestException extends GeneralSecurityException {
  public DigestException() {
  }
  public DigestException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

class MessageDigest extends Object {
  /* Which block function this instance runs. The two 64-bit SHA-2 sizes share
     one block function and differ in their initial value and their output
     length, exactly as they do in the standards. */
  private static final int MD5 = 0
  private static final int SHA1 = 1
  private static final int SHA224 = 2
  private static final int SHA256 = 3
  private static final int SHA384 = 4
  private static final int SHA512 = 5

  /* MD5, RFC 1321: T[i] is floor(2^32 * abs(sin(i + 1))) and S[i] the per-round
     rotation. These are arrays rather than named constants for a reason worth
     writing down: a `static final int` initialized in place from a hexadecimal
     literal over 2^31 folds to the literal's own width, so the field reads back
     as 3614090360 rather than -680876936, while an initializer inside an array
     (and every other kind of int field) does truncate. Keeping the standards'
     constants in the notation the standards write them in therefore means
     keeping them in a table, which is where they belong anyway. */
  private static final int[] MD5_K = {
    0xd76aa478, 0xe8c7b756, 0x242070db, 0xc1bdceee, 0xf57c0faf, 0x4787c62a,
    0xa8304613, 0xfd469501, 0x698098d8, 0x8b44f7af, 0xffff5bb1, 0x895cd7be,
    0x6b901122, 0xfd987193, 0xa679438e, 0x49b40821, 0xf61e2562, 0xc040b340,
    0x265e5a51, 0xe9b6c7aa, 0xd62f105d, 0x02441453, 0xd8a1e681, 0xe7d3fbc8,
    0x21e1cde6, 0xc33707d6, 0xf4d50d87, 0x455a14ed, 0xa9e3e905, 0xfcefa3f8,
    0x676f02d9, 0x8d2a4c8a, 0xfffa3942, 0x8771f681, 0x6d9d6122, 0xfde5380c,
    0xa4beea44, 0x4bdecfa9, 0xf6bb4b60, 0xbebfbc70, 0x289b7ec6, 0xeaa127fa,
    0xd4ef3085, 0x04881d05, 0xd9d4d039, 0xe6db99e5, 0x1fa27cf8, 0xc4ac5665,
    0xf4292244, 0x432aff97, 0xab9423a7, 0xfc93a039, 0x655b59c3, 0x8f0ccc92,
    0xffeff47d, 0x85845dd1, 0x6fa87e4f, 0xfe2ce6e0, 0xa3014314, 0x4e0811a1,
    0xf7537e82, 0xbd3af235, 0x2ad7d2bb, 0xeb86d391,
  }
  private static final int[] MD5_S = {
    7, 12, 17, 22, 7, 12, 17, 22, 7, 12, 17, 22, 7, 12, 17, 22,
    5, 9, 14, 20, 5, 9, 14, 20, 5, 9, 14, 20, 5, 9, 14, 20,
    4, 11, 16, 23, 4, 11, 16, 23, 4, 11, 16, 23, 4, 11, 16, 23,
    6, 10, 15, 21, 6, 10, 15, 21, 6, 10, 15, 21, 6, 10, 15, 21,
  }
  private static final int[] MD5_IV = { 0x67452301, 0xefcdab89, 0x98badcfe, 0x10325476 }

  /* SHA-1, FIPS 180-4: five words, eighty rounds, four constants. */
  private static final int[] SHA1_IV = { 0x67452301, 0xefcdab89, 0x98badcfe, 0x10325476, 0xc3d2e1f0 }
  private static final int[] SHA1_K = { 0x5a827999, 0x6ed9eba1, 0x8f1bbcdc, 0xca62c1d6 }

  /* SHA-256 and SHA-224: eight words, sixty-four rounds. SHA-224 is SHA-256 with
     the second thirty-two bits of the square roots of the ninth to sixteenth
     primes as its initial value and its last word left unprinted, which is why
     one implementation serves both. */
  private static final int[] SHA256_IV = {
    0x6a09e667, 0xbb67ae85, 0x3c6ef372, 0xa54ff53a, 0x510e527f, 0x9b05688c, 0x1f83d9ab, 0x5be0cd19,
  }
  private static final int[] SHA224_IV = {
    0xc1059ed8, 0x367cd507, 0x3070dd17, 0xf70e5939, 0xffc00b31, 0x68581511, 0x64f98fa7, 0xbefa4fa4,
  }
  private static final int[] SHA256_K = {
    0x428a2f98, 0x71374491, 0xb5c0fbcf, 0xe9b5dba5, 0x3956c25b, 0x59f111f1,
    0x923f82a4, 0xab1c5ed5, 0xd807aa98, 0x12835b01, 0x243185be, 0x550c7dc3,
    0x72be5d74, 0x80deb1fe, 0x9bdc06a7, 0xc19bf174, 0xe49b69c1, 0xefbe4786,
    0x0fc19dc6, 0x240ca1cc, 0x2de92c6f, 0x4a7484aa, 0x5cb0a9dc, 0x76f988da,
    0x983e5152, 0xa831c66d, 0xb00327c8, 0xbf597fc7, 0xc6e00bf3, 0xd5a79147,
    0x06ca6351, 0x14292967, 0x27b70a85, 0x2e1b2138, 0x4d2c6dfc, 0x53380d13,
    0x650a7354, 0x766a0abb, 0x81c2c92e, 0x92722c85, 0xa2bfe8a1, 0xa81a664b,
    0xc24b8b70, 0xc76c51a3, 0xd192e819, 0xd6990624, 0xf40e3585, 0x106aa070,
    0x19a4c116, 0x1e376c08, 0x2748774c, 0x34b0bcb5, 0x391c0cb3, 0x4ed8aa4a,
    0x5b9cca4f, 0x682e6ff3, 0x748f82ee, 0x78a5636f, 0x84c87814, 0x8cc70208,
    0x90befffa, 0xa4506ceb, 0xbef9a3f7, 0xc67178f2,
  }

  /* SHA-512 and SHA-384: eight 64-bit words, eighty rounds, eighty constants.
     SHA-384 is SHA-512 with the square roots of the ninth to sixteenth primes
     as its initial value and its last two words left unprinted. */
  private static final long[] SHA512_IV = {
    0x6a09e667f3bcc908L, 0xbb67ae8584caa73bL, 0x3c6ef372fe94f82bL, 0xa54ff53a5f1d36f1L,
    0x510e527fade682d1L, 0x9b05688c2b3e6c1fL, 0x1f83d9abfb41bd6bL, 0x5be0cd19137e2179L,
  }
  private static final long[] SHA384_IV = {
    0xcbbb9d5dc1059ed8L, 0x629a292a367cd507L, 0x9159015a3070dd17L, 0x152fecd8f70e5939L,
    0x67332667ffc00b31L, 0x8eb44a8768581511L, 0xdb0c2e0d64f98fa7L, 0x47b5481dbefa4fa4L,
  }
  private static final long[] SHA512_K = {
    0x428a2f98d728ae22L, 0x7137449123ef65cdL, 0xb5c0fbcfec4d3b2fL, 0xe9b5dba58189dbbcL,
    0x3956c25bf348b538L, 0x59f111f1b605d019L, 0x923f82a4af194f9bL, 0xab1c5ed5da6d8118L,
    0xd807aa98a3030242L, 0x12835b0145706fbeL, 0x243185be4ee4b28cL, 0x550c7dc3d5ffb4e2L,
    0x72be5d74f27b896fL, 0x80deb1fe3b1696b1L, 0x9bdc06a725c71235L, 0xc19bf174cf692694L,
    0xe49b69c19ef14ad2L, 0xefbe4786384f25e3L, 0x0fc19dc68b8cd5b5L, 0x240ca1cc77ac9c65L,
    0x2de92c6f592b0275L, 0x4a7484aa6ea6e483L, 0x5cb0a9dcbd41fbd4L, 0x76f988da831153b5L,
    0x983e5152ee66dfabL, 0xa831c66d2db43210L, 0xb00327c898fb213fL, 0xbf597fc7beef0ee4L,
    0xc6e00bf33da88fc2L, 0xd5a79147930aa725L, 0x06ca6351e003826fL, 0x142929670a0e6e70L,
    0x27b70a8546d22ffcL, 0x2e1b21385c26c926L, 0x4d2c6dfc5ac42aedL, 0x53380d139d95b3dfL,
    0x650a73548baf63deL, 0x766a0abb3c77b2a8L, 0x81c2c92e47edaee6L, 0x92722c851482353bL,
    0xa2bfe8a14cf10364L, 0xa81a664bbc423001L, 0xc24b8b70d0f89791L, 0xc76c51a30654be30L,
    0xd192e819d6ef5218L, 0xd69906245565a910L, 0xf40e35855771202aL, 0x106aa07032bbd1b8L,
    0x19a4c116b8d2d0c8L, 0x1e376c085141ab53L, 0x2748774cdf8eeb99L, 0x34b0bcb5e19b48a8L,
    0x391c0cb3c5c95a63L, 0x4ed8aa4ae3418acbL, 0x5b9cca4f7763e373L, 0x682e6ff3d6b2b8a3L,
    0x748f82ee5defb2fcL, 0x78a5636f43172f60L, 0x84c87814a1f0ab72L, 0x8cc702081a6439ecL,
    0x90befffa23631e28L, 0xa4506cebde82bde9L, 0xbef9a3f7b2c67915L, 0xc67178f2e372532bL,
    0xca273eceea26619cL, 0xd186b8c721c0c207L, 0xeada7dd6cde0eb1eL, 0xf57d4f7fee6ed178L,
    0x06f067aa72176fbaL, 0x0a637dc5a2c898a6L, 0x113f9804bef90daeL, 0x1b710b35131c471bL,
    0x28db77f523047d84L, 0x32caab7b40c72493L, 0x3c9ebe0a15c9bebcL, 0x431d67c49c100d4cL,
    0x4cc5d4becb3e42b6L, 0x597f299cfc657e2aL, 0x5fcb6fab3ad6faecL, 0x6c44198c4a475817L,
  }

  /* The name the caller asked for, which is what getAlgorithm answers: Java
     does not canonicalize it either, so getInstance("md5").getAlgorithm() is
     "md5" and not "MD5". */
  private String algorithm
  private int kind

  /* The state. The 32-bit block functions keep theirs in h32 -- four words for
     MD5, five for SHA-1, eight for the SHA-2 pair -- and the 64-bit pair in
     h64; only the array the current kind uses is ever read. */
  private int[] h32
  private long[] h64

  /* The bytes of the message that do not complete a block yet, and how many of
     them there are. The block is 64 bytes for the 32-bit kinds and 128 for the
     64-bit ones. */
  private byte[] buf
  private int bufLen

  /* The message length in bytes. Every one of these five standards ends its
     message with the bit length: 64 bits of it for MD5, SHA-1 and SHA-256, and
     128 bits of it for SHA-512 and SHA-384. A byte count is all that has to be
     kept for either, because the bit count is that count shifted -- a byte
     count that is a long already carries all 128 bits of the bit count for
     SHA-512, in its own top three bits. */
  private long total

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------ the bits */

  private static int rotl32(int x, int n) {
    return (x << n) | (x >>> (32 - n))
  }
  private static int rotr32(int x, int n) {
    return (x >>> n) | (x << (32 - n))
  }
  private static long rotr64(long x, int n) {
    return (x >>> n) | (x << (64 - n))
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- the structure */

  private int blockSize() {
    if (kind == SHA384 || kind == SHA512) {
      return 128
    }
    return 64
  }

  /* The initial value of the current kind, which is both the state a fresh
     digest starts in and the one reset() returns to. */
  private void init() {
    h32 = new int[8]
    h64 = new long[8]
    buf = new byte[blockSize()]
    bufLen = 0
    total = 0
    if (kind == MD5) {
      for (int i = 0 : i < 4 : i++) {
        h32[i] = MD5_IV[i]
      }
      return
    }
    if (kind == SHA1) {
      for (int i = 0 : i < 5 : i++) {
        h32[i] = SHA1_IV[i]
      }
      return
    }
    if (kind == SHA224 || kind == SHA256) {
      int[] iv = SHA256_IV
      if (kind == SHA224) {
        iv = SHA224_IV
      }
      for (int i = 0 : i < 8 : i++) {
        h32[i] = iv[i]
      }
      return
    }
    long[] iv = SHA512_IV
    if (kind == SHA384) {
      iv = SHA384_IV
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < 8 : i++) {
      h64[i] = iv[i]
    }
  }

  /* One block, from `off` in `b`, folded into the state. Every call site hands
     a whole block: the buffer when the message ended inside a block, and the
     caller's array when it did not. */
  private void block(byte[] b, int off) {
    if (kind == MD5) {
      md5Block(b, off)
      return
    }
    if (kind == SHA1) {
      sha1Block(b, off)
      return
    }
    if (kind == SHA384 || kind == SHA512) {
      sha512Block(b, off)
      return
    }
    sha256Block(b, off)
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------- the 32-bit functions */

  private void md5Block(byte[] b, int off) {
    int[] x = new int[16]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 16 : i++) {
      int j = off + i * 4
      x[i] = (b[j] & 0xFF) | ((b[j + 1] & 0xFF) << 8) | ((b[j + 2] & 0xFF) << 16) | ((b[j + 3] & 0xFF) << 24)
    }
    int a = h32[0]
    int bb = h32[1]
    int c = h32[2]
    int d = h32[3]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 64 : i++) {
      int f = 0
      int g = 0
      if (i < 16) {
        f = (bb & c) | (~bb & d)
        g = i
      } else if (i < 32) {
        f = (d & bb) | (~d & c)
        g = (5 * i + 1) % 16
      } else if (i < 48) {
        f = bb ^ c ^ d
        g = (3 * i + 5) % 16
      } else {
        f = c ^ (bb | ~d)
        g = (7 * i) % 16
      }
      int tmp = d
      d = c
      c = bb
      bb = bb + rotl32(a + f + MD5_K[i] + x[g], MD5_S[i])
      a = tmp
    }
    h32[0] = h32[0] + a
    h32[1] = h32[1] + bb
    h32[2] = h32[2] + c
    h32[3] = h32[3] + d
  }

  private void sha1Block(byte[] b, int off) {
    int[] w = new int[80]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 16 : i++) {
      int j = off + i * 4
      w[i] = ((b[j] & 0xFF) << 24) | ((b[j + 1] & 0xFF) << 16) | ((b[j + 2] & 0xFF) << 8) | (b[j + 3] & 0xFF)
    }
    for (int i = 16 : i < 80 : i++) {
      w[i] = rotl32(w[i - 3] ^ w[i - 8] ^ w[i - 14] ^ w[i - 16], 1)
    }
    int a = h32[0]
    int bb = h32[1]
    int c = h32[2]
    int d = h32[3]
    int e = h32[4]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 80 : i++) {
      int f = 0
      int k = 0
      if (i < 20) {
        f = (bb & c) | (~bb & d)
        k = SHA1_K[0]
      } else if (i < 40) {
        f = bb ^ c ^ d
        k = SHA1_K[1]
      } else if (i < 60) {
        f = (bb & c) | (bb & d) | (c & d)
        k = SHA1_K[2]
      } else {
        f = bb ^ c ^ d
        k = SHA1_K[3]
      }
      int tmp = rotl32(a, 5) + f + e + k + w[i]
      e = d
      d = c
      c = rotl32(bb, 30)
      bb = a
      a = tmp
    }
    h32[0] = h32[0] + a
    h32[1] = h32[1] + bb
    h32[2] = h32[2] + c
    h32[3] = h32[3] + d
    h32[4] = h32[4] + e
  }

  private void sha256Block(byte[] b, int off) {
    int[] w = new int[64]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 16 : i++) {
      int j = off + i * 4
      w[i] = ((b[j] & 0xFF) << 24) | ((b[j + 1] & 0xFF) << 16) | ((b[j + 2] & 0xFF) << 8) | (b[j + 3] & 0xFF)
    }
    for (int i = 16 : i < 64 : i++) {
      int x = w[i - 15]
      int y = w[i - 2]
      int s0 = rotr32(x, 7) ^ rotr32(x, 18) ^ (x >>> 3)
      int s1 = rotr32(y, 17) ^ rotr32(y, 19) ^ (y >>> 10)
      w[i] = w[i - 16] + s0 + w[i - 7] + s1
    }
    int a = h32[0]
    int bb = h32[1]
    int c = h32[2]
    int d = h32[3]
    int e = h32[4]
    int f = h32[5]
    int g = h32[6]
    int h = h32[7]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 64 : i++) {
      int s1 = rotr32(e, 6) ^ rotr32(e, 11) ^ rotr32(e, 25)
      int ch = (e & f) ^ (~e & g)
      int t1 = h + s1 + ch + SHA256_K[i] + w[i]
      int s0 = rotr32(a, 2) ^ rotr32(a, 13) ^ rotr32(a, 22)
      int maj = (a & bb) ^ (a & c) ^ (bb & c)
      int t2 = s0 + maj
      h = g
      g = f
      f = e
      e = d + t1
      d = c
      c = bb
      bb = a
      a = t1 + t2
    }
    h32[0] = h32[0] + a
    h32[1] = h32[1] + bb
    h32[2] = h32[2] + c
    h32[3] = h32[3] + d
    h32[4] = h32[4] + e
    h32[5] = h32[5] + f
    h32[6] = h32[6] + g
    h32[7] = h32[7] + h
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------- the 64-bit functions */

  private void sha512Block(byte[] b, int off) {
    long[] w = new long[80]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 16 : i++) {
      int j = off + i * 8
      w[i] = ((b[j] & 0xFFL) << 56) | ((b[j + 1] & 0xFFL) << 48) | ((b[j + 2] & 0xFFL) << 40) | ((b[j + 3] & 0xFFL) << 32) |
             ((b[j + 4] & 0xFFL) << 24) | ((b[j + 5] & 0xFFL) << 16) | ((b[j + 6] & 0xFFL) << 8) | (b[j + 7] & 0xFFL)
    }
    for (int i = 16 : i < 80 : i++) {
      long x = w[i - 15]
      long y = w[i - 2]
      long s0 = rotr64(x, 1) ^ rotr64(x, 8) ^ (x >>> 7)
      long s1 = rotr64(y, 19) ^ rotr64(y, 61) ^ (y >>> 6)
      w[i] = w[i - 16] + s0 + w[i - 7] + s1
    }
    long a = h64[0]
    long bb = h64[1]
    long c = h64[2]
    long d = h64[3]
    long e = h64[4]
    long f = h64[5]
    long g = h64[6]
    long h = h64[7]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 80 : i++) {
      long s1 = rotr64(e, 14) ^ rotr64(e, 18) ^ rotr64(e, 41)
      long ch = (e & f) ^ (~e & g)
      long t1 = h + s1 + ch + SHA512_K[i] + w[i]
      long s0 = rotr64(a, 28) ^ rotr64(a, 34) ^ rotr64(a, 39)
      long maj = (a & bb) ^ (a & c) ^ (bb & c)
      long t2 = s0 + maj
      h = g
      g = f
      f = e
      e = d + t1
      d = c
      c = bb
      bb = a
      a = t1 + t2
    }
    h64[0] = h64[0] + a
    h64[1] = h64[1] + bb
    h64[2] = h64[2] + c
    h64[3] = h64[3] + d
    h64[4] = h64[4] + e
    h64[5] = h64[5] + f
    h64[6] = h64[6] + g
    h64[7] = h64[7] + h
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- the finishing */

  /* RFC 1321 and FIPS 180-4 each say the same thing about the end of a message:
     a single 1 bit, then zeros until the length field would fit, then the
     message's length in bits -- little-endian for MD5, big-endian for the SHA
     family, and 128 bits wide for the 64-bit pair. */
  private void finish() {
    long bits = total << 3
    int bs = blockSize()
    int lenField = 8
    if (kind == SHA384 || kind == SHA512) {
      lenField = 16
    }
    buf[bufLen] = (byte) 0x80
    bufLen++
    if (bufLen > bs - lenField) {
      while (bufLen < bs) {
        buf[bufLen] = (byte) 0
        bufLen++
      }
      block(buf, 0)
      bufLen = 0
    }
    while (bufLen < bs - lenField) {
      buf[bufLen] = (byte) 0
      bufLen++
    }
    if (lenField == 16) {
      long high = total >>> 61
      for (int i = 0 : i < 8 : i++) {
        buf[bufLen + i] = (byte) (high >>> (56 - 8 * i))
      }
      bufLen = bufLen + 8
    }
    if (kind == MD5) {
      for (int i = 0 : i < 8 : i++) {
        buf[bufLen + i] = (byte) (bits >>> (8 * i))
      }
    } else {
      for (int i = 0 : i < 8 : i++) {
        buf[bufLen + i] = (byte) (bits >>> (56 - 8 * i))
      }
    }
    block(buf, 0)
    bufLen = 0
  }

  /* The state as the digest bytes: the four MD5 words little-endian and in
     order, the SHA-1 and SHA-256 words big-endian, the SHA-512 words
     big-endian -- and for SHA-224 and SHA-384 only as many words as the
     standard prints, which is where their lengths come from. */
  private byte[] stateBytes() {
    byte[] out = new byte[digestLength()]
    if (kind == MD5) {
      for (int i = 0 : i < 4 : i++) {
        int v = h32[i]
        for (int j = 0 : j < 4 : j++) {
          out[i * 4 + j] = (byte) (v >>> (8 * j))
        }
      }
      return out
    }
    if (kind == SHA384 || kind == SHA512) {
      int words = 8
      if (kind == SHA384) {
        words = 6
      }
      for (int i = 0 : i < words : i++) {
        long v = h64[i]
        for (int j = 0 : j < 8 : j++) {
          out[i * 8 + j] = (byte) (v >>> (56 - 8 * j))
        }
      }
      return out
    }
    int words = 8
    if (kind == SHA1) {
      words = 5
    }
    if (kind == SHA224) {
      words = 7
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < words : i++) {
      int v = h32[i]
      for (int j = 0 : j < 4 : j++) {
        out[i * 4 + j] = (byte) (v >>> (24 - 8 * j))
      }
    }
    return out
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------ the shape */

  private MessageDigest(int kind, String algorithm) {
    this.kind = kind
    this.algorithm = algorithm
    init()
  }

  /* Java's aliases, read off the JDK's own provider: SHA for SHA-1, SHA1 for
     SHA-1, and the hyphenless spelling of every SHA-2 name. The lookup is
     case-insensitive, as Java's is, and the name is not canonicalized on the
     way in, because Java does not canonicalize it either. */
  public static MessageDigest getInstance(String algorithm) {
    if (algorithm == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("null algorithm name")
    }
    if (algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("MD5")) {
      return new MessageDigest(MD5, algorithm)
    }
    if (algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA") || algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA-1") || algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA1")) {
      return new MessageDigest(SHA1, algorithm)
    }
    if (algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA-224") || algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA224")) {
      return new MessageDigest(SHA224, algorithm)
    }
    if (algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA-256") || algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA256")) {
      return new MessageDigest(SHA256, algorithm)
    }
    if (algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA-384") || algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA384")) {
      return new MessageDigest(SHA384, algorithm)
    }
    if (algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA-512") || algorithm.equalsIgnoreCase("SHA512")) {
      return new MessageDigest(SHA512, algorithm)
    }
    throw new NoSuchAlgorithmException(algorithm + " MessageDigest not available")
  }

  public String getAlgorithm() {
    return algorithm
  }

  public int getDigestLength() {
    return digestLength()
  }

  private int digestLength() {
    if (kind == MD5) {
      return 16
    }
    if (kind == SHA1) {
      return 20
    }
    if (kind == SHA224) {
      return 28
    }
    if (kind == SHA256) {
      return 32
    }
    if (kind == SHA384) {
      return 48
    }
    return 64
  }

  /* The name the provider would have built this digest under, which is what
     Java's DigestException carries: the JDK's MessageDigest passes the
     caller's spelling to nothing but getAlgorithm(), and the provider's own
     canonical name to the one message that names the algorithm. So
     getInstance("sha1").getAlgorithm() is "sha1" while the exception below it
     says "SHA-1", and both of those are Java's behaviour, not a slip. */
  private String canonicalName() {
    if (kind == MD5) {
      return "MD5"
    }
    if (kind == SHA1) {
      return "SHA-1"
    }
    if (kind == SHA224) {
      return "SHA-224"
    }
    if (kind == SHA256) {
      return "SHA-256"
    }
    if (kind == SHA384) {
      return "SHA-384"
    }
    return "SHA-512"
  }

  public void reset() {
    init()
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------ the input */

  public void update(byte input) {
    buf[bufLen] = input
    bufLen++
    total++
    if (bufLen == blockSize()) {
      block(buf, 0)
      bufLen = 0
    }
  }

  public void update(byte[] input) {
    if (input == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    update(input, 0, input.length)
  }

  /* A message is folded in as it arrives: whatever completes a block is
     processed straight out of the caller's array, and only the tail that does
     not complete one is copied into the buffer. */
  public void update(byte[] input, int offset, int len) {
    if (input == null) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("No input buffer given")
    }
    /* Java's own two checks, in its order: the length check is on the pair as
       MessageDigest sees them, and the range check that follows is the one the
       provider makes with jdk.internal.util.Preconditions, which reports the
       half-open range it refused. Both are reproduced, message and all, so a
       program that prints one prints what javac's program printed. */
    if (input.length - offset < len) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input buffer too short")
    }
    if (offset < 0 || len < 0 || len > input.length - offset) {
      throw new ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException("Range [" + offset + ", " + offset + " + " + len +
        ") out of bounds for length " + input.length)
    }
    int bs = blockSize()
    total = total + len
    int pos = offset
    int end = offset + len
    if (bufLen > 0) {
      while (pos < end && bufLen < bs) {
        buf[bufLen] = input[pos]
        bufLen++
        pos++
      }
      if (bufLen == bs) {
        block(buf, 0)
        bufLen = 0
      }
    }
    while (end - pos >= bs) {
      block(input, pos)
      pos = pos + bs
    }
    while (pos < end) {
      buf[bufLen] = input[pos]
      bufLen++
      pos++
    }
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------- the output */

  /* digest() completes the message, hands back its digest and returns the
     object to its initial state, which is what Java's digest() does: a caller
     who wants to hash twice reuses the digest rather than constructing one. */
  public byte[] digest() {
    finish()
    byte[] out = stateBytes()
    reset()
    return out
  }

  public byte[] digest(byte[] input) {
    update(input)
    return digest()
  }

  /* Java's buffer-shaped digest: the digest goes into the caller's array at
     `offset`, and what comes back is how many bytes were written. The two
     failures are Java's, message for message, including the missing space
     before "digests" that the JDK's own string has. */
  public int digest(byte[] out, int offset, int len) {
    if (out == null) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("No output buffer given")
    }
    if (out.length - offset < len) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("Output buffer too small for specified offset and length")
    }
    if (len < digestLength()) {
      throw new DigestException("Length must be at least " + digestLength() + " for " + canonicalName() + "digests")
    }
    byte[] d = digest()
    System.arraycopy(d, 0, out, offset, d.length)
    return d.length
  }

  /* The comparison a program should use on two digests, and the reason it
     exists rather than equals: it does not stop at the first difference, so
     how long it takes says nothing about how much of the digest matched. */
  public static boolean isEqual(byte[] digesta, byte[] digestb) {
    if (digesta == digestb) {
      return true
    }
    if (digesta == null || digestb == null) {
      return false
    }
    if (digesta.length != digestb.length) {
      return false
    }
    int result = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < digesta.length : i++) {
      result = result | (digesta[i] ^ digestb[i])
    }
    return result == 0
  }
}

/* java.util.zip.Checksum: what a checksum is asked for by the classes that
   stream bytes through one. Java declares update(byte[]) as a default method on
   the interface -- the two three-argument and one-byte forms are the ones an
   implementation must write -- and that is how it is written here. */
interface Checksum {
  void update(int b)
  default void update(byte[] b) {
    update(b, 0, b.length)
  }
  void update(byte[] b, int off, int len)
  long getValue()
  void reset()
}

/* java.util.zip.CRC32, the reflected CRC-32 that gzip, zip and PNG all use: the
   polynomial is 0xEDB88320 (0x04C11DB7 reversed), the register starts at all
   ones and the value is complemented on the way out, which is why the empty
   message checksums to zero.

   The table is built once from the polynomial rather than written out; a
   256-entry table of numbers nobody reads is 256 chances to mistype a digit,
   and the loop that produces it is the definition. */
class CRC32 extends Object implements Checksum {
  private static int[] table() {
    int[] t = new int[256]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 256 : i++) {
      int c = i
      for (int k = 0 : k < 8 : k++) {
        if ((c & 1) != 0) {
          c = (int) (0xEDB88320L ^ (c >>> 1))
        } else {
          c = c >>> 1
        }
      }
      t[i] = c
    }
    return t
  }
  private static final int[] TABLE = table()

  /* The running register, in the complemented form the algorithm keeps it in:
     it starts at all ones, every byte mixes in at the bottom, and getValue
     complements it back out. Only getValue() is observable -- Java's own field
     holds the same 2^32 states in a different arrangement, and this one reaches
     the same value. */
  private int crc = -1

  public CRC32() {
  }

  public void update(int b) {
    crc = (crc >>> 8) ^ TABLE[(crc ^ b) & 0xFF]
  }

  public void update(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    if (b == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    /* Java's check here is the same Preconditions call the digest makes, so
       the message that names the refused range is the same message. */
    if (off < 0 || len < 0 || len > b.length - off) {
      throw new ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException("Range [" + off + ", " + off + " + " + len +
        ") out of bounds for length " + b.length)
    }
    int end = off + len
    for (int i = off : i < end : i++) {
      crc = (crc >>> 8) ^ TABLE[(crc ^ b[i]) & 0xFF]
    }
  }

  public long getValue() {
    return (crc ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) & 0xFFFFFFFFL
  }

  public void reset() {
    crc = -1
  }
}
